WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.669 align:start position:0%
 
they were scenes that  gripped the world's attention for 18  straight days  millions of egyptians demonstrated in  the streets and squares of the country's  cities  ultimately bringing about the end of  president hosni robotics 30-year rule  the protests began on january the 25th  with polls and social media platforms  for people to take to the streets  demanding freedom justice and bread  [Music]  those calls by unarmed civilians were  met with a type of police brutality that  was synonymous with the mubarak regime  consequently the demands and chance  shifted to a simple  and very clear one  the people demand the fall of the regime  on february the 11th it fell or so they  thought  mubarak stepped aside and the country's  military generals took charge  despite attempts by the army to maintain  power they were forced to allow the  country's first ever free and fair  parliamentary elections to take place  those were then followed by a vote for  the presidency which resulted in the  muslim brotherhood party's muhammad  mursi  becoming egypt's first ever  democratically elected president  throughout that time social and economic  instability became the norm  egyptians had begun experiencing  political freedom but living conditions  remained bad regular electricity outages  and the never-ending fuel shortage  crisis  led many to direct their opposition to  egypt's post-revolution president  this content continued to rise and in  2013  supported by the army and police another  mass protest took place  albeit far smaller in size and shorter  in duration than that of 2011.  the protesters said mursi had to go  because he failed to deliver on the  goals of the revolution  his supporters said it was the  counter-revolution that had been  sabotaging the morsi presidency  and was now behind the unrest it later  transpired that most of the crises were  in fact  manufactured and the violence which  rocked egypt's streets  had been encouraged by a deep state that  wanted to reverse the gains of the  january 25th revolution  the removal of morsi by a military coup  in 2013 installed army general abdel  fattah sisi as leader and ultimately  president  since al-sisi came to power egypt has  become the world's second largest  borrower from the imf  in 2014 the country had a foreign debt  of 46 billion dollars  today that figure is more than 125  billion  poverty levels in egypt have increased  drastically and more than 50 percent of  the country's revenue is spent  on trying to pay back its foreign debt  meanwhile al-sisi has found money to  build several huge new palaces  and buy a new presidential plane amongst  other things  actions that have enraged the egyptian  public the vast majority of which  lives on or below the poverty line  ten years on cairo's tahrir square is a  far cry from what it looked like  egypt is not the country those who  protested dreamed it would become  and for tens of millions of egyptians  they have little if any  freedom justice and bread  well jamal al-shayal joins us now live  in doha jamal  you reported on the arab spring closely  particularly egypt what can you remember  of that time  well it's very difficult to put into  words really what  we witnessed back then on january 25th  and the uh ensuing couple of weeks after  you know  when we talk about historical moments  moments that changed  the world definitely the 18 days  in tahrir and across egypt actually  were one of those things when you're  talking about millions of people coming  together  um with a very clear goal or demand  um you know waves upon waves of people  the way in which they  uh you know faced uh bullets with their  bare chests the way in which they united  regardless of their sex or religion  uh age gender all of these things they  were really uh  breathtaking scenes but what i remember  also as a journalist from that  perspective was just  how uh challenging it was to try and  uh essentially convey the message of  these people convey their  uh suffering convey their aspirations um  in you know short bite-sized two-minute  three-minute reports  reporting you're talking about 90  million egyptians  many of whom had lived 30 years under  this autocratic rule  and were finally essentially exploding  with a desire for a better way of life  so it was it was definitely historic  historic  assignment as you outlined in your  package there's been  even more political upheaval since then  how would you say the country is faring  now  well we just need to look at the facts  right so 10 years on you have  dozens of journalists behind bars  including one of our colleagues mahmoud  hussein who's been  imprisoned by the egyptian authorities  for some 500 days  you have poverty levels that are even  worse than they were  under mubarak so roughly 60 of the  egyptian population on  or below the poverty line um you have  egypt's  political standing in the region uh you  know  rolling back from being a leader to  being one  that has its policies dictated by many  states like the uae and others and  all of that basically means that those  who  revolted uh during the 2011 uprising  have seen not only that they  unfortunately for them  failed to achieve their demands and  their goals  but in fact the clock has been set back  in many respects  to worse than it was under mubarak but  that being said it doesn't mean that  they will have  they will admit that the revolution has  failed they've many people view it as  uh you know one round that they lost um  and  the proof in that is that there are  still mobilizations that take place in  sporadic protests  against the military against sisi's rule  that happen every so often in egypt that  many predict will culminate into  another round of mass uprisings in the  coming  months if not maybe a couple of years a  bleak outlook many thanks for that jamal  al-shail deforest live in doha

